          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"zuccoli-lamore-di-loredana-9a817d5f2cd7e04d\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Filippo directly avows that he loves Loredana intensely. The utterance presents a loving state as true, even though the surrounding narration casts him as a liar. It does not itself undertake a commitment, and its affectionate, sexually charged delivery supplies no independent evidence of expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The phrase “che mentiva” may implicate this avowal as well as Filippo’s preceding oath, but possible insincerity does not alter its truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Filippo directly says to Loredana, “io ti amo tanto” (“I love you so much”), while drawing her toward the bed.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal follows Filippo’s knowingly false denial that his family wants him to marry and his false word of honor. It helps soothe and seduce the distressed Loredana, while the narrator’s description of him as one who “lied and kissed so well” creates irony and doubt about sincerity. Nevertheless, the subsequent narration says that opposition from society and his relatives had made Loredana dearer to him, so his affection need not be wholly fabricated. These complications concern truthfulness and interpersonal use rather than introducing performative or reflexive core force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Tu sei tanto bella, io ti amo tanto....”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense first-person avowal strongly supports truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Filippo knowingly gives a false denial and his “parola d’onore”; immediately afterward the narrator calls him one “che mentiva e baciava così bene.”\",\"supports\":\"This casts doubt on sincerity and highlights the utterance’s soothing or seductive contextual role, without changing its avowal mode or establishing a commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Filippo laughs, embraces Loredana, lifts her, praises her beauty, and lays her on the bed as she seeks his mouth through tears.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal occurs in an affectionate and erotic interaction, but these delivery circumstances do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The following chapter states that social gossip and his relatives’ hostility “gli avevan reso la fanciulla anche più cara” and that he often gave up entertainments to spend time with her.\",\"supports\":\"The wider supplied passage leaves room for genuine affection despite Filippo’s deception about marriage.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as affirmative present tense, main-or-embedded form, and reviewed as VALID_EXPLICIT_LOVE_I_YOU.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted occurrence is an explicit affirmative love utterance rather than a merely related expression.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Deception, reassurance, and seduction are contextual features; no important core-force residue requires P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
